; symbols-wire.fk — the .fkb wire door for symbol tables, split out of
; symbols.fk 2026-08-28.
;
; preludes: form-stdlib/symbols.fk
;
; WHY THIS IS ITS OWN CELL. symbol-table-to-wire / symbol-table-from-wire are
; the only two things in the symbol layer that reach recipe_to_bytes /
; bytes_to_recipe, and those two are native on go / rust / ts and ABSENT on
; fkwu. Because they were named inside symbols.fk, every chain that preluded
; symbols.fk inherited the absence as two unresolved calls — and an unresolved
; call answers `nothing` quietly under axiom-5, so a band's bits that touch one
; read 0 without the band saying so. Eighteen cells prelude symbols.fk; two of
; them call the wire door.
;
; This is the repair cache-binary.fk made for read_form_binary /
; write_form_binary on 2026-08-14, made once at the source instead of once per
; consumer: a cell should not hand its callers a door they did not ask for and
; cannot open on their own arm. The door is not smaller — it is where its
; callers reach for it.
;
; Callers today: form-stdlib/tests/symbols-band.fk and
; form-samples/cross-modal/24-wire-binary-symbols/wire-binary-symbols.fk.

;; (symbol-table-to-wire table) → byte-list ready to cross any channel.
;; Inverse of (symbol-table-from-wire bytes).
(defn symbol-table-to-wire (table)
    (recipe_to_bytes table))

;; (symbol-table-from-wire bytes) → local SYMBOL-TABLE NodeID.
;; The receiving cell calls this on bytes received from a sender.
;; Content-addressing makes the local NodeID structurally identical
;; to the sender's — looking up the same name in either cell's table
;; returns the same value-Recipe.
(defn symbol-table-from-wire (bytes)
    (bytes_to_recipe bytes))
